# Xwayland -- an X server that renders into a Wayland compositor, so that X11
# applications keep working on a Wayland-only desktop.
#
# It is the densest concentration of silent-skip gates in this tree. Its
# meson.build carries TWELVE options defaulting to `auto` plus two lookups that
# are `required: false` with no option at all, and almost none of them fail when
# the thing they look for is missing -- they produce a different Xwayland and
# report success. Four of those decide something a user would notice, and the
# reason this file is long is that each one had to be found by reading the build
# file rather than the option list.
#
# BLFS 12.4 / GNOME 48, pinned here rather than in versions.env: versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh, so a desktop
# pin placed there is destroyed by the next `make pin`.
SRC_URL=https://www.x.org/pub/individual/xserver/xwayland-24.1.8.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=c8908d57c8ed9ceb8293c16ba7ad5af522efaf1ba7e51f9e4cf3c0774d199907
SRC_DIR=xwayland-24.1.8

# ===========================================================================
# THE FOUR THAT MATTER
# ===========================================================================
#
# 1. -Dsha1=libgcrypt. THE OPTION NOBODY WOULD THINK TO LOOK AT, and it is not
#    optional in any sense: os/xsha1.c is compiled unconditionally into
#    libxserver_os (os/meson.build:19), and meson.build 307-314 error()s when no
#    provider is found. Under `auto` the search is a FIRST MATCH over eight
#    candidates in a fixed order (meson.build 228-240):
#
#        libc, CommonCrypto, CryptoAPI, libmd, libsha1, libnettle,
#        libgcrypt, libcrypto
#
#    which is the same shape as libei's sd-bus provider and pipewire's logind
#    provider. glibc has no SHA1Init, libmd/libsha1/libnettle are not packaged,
#    so the first hit here is libgcrypt -- an entire cryptographic library
#    linked into the X server by a default nobody wrote down.
#
#    NAMING IT RATHER THAN LETTING IT LAND THERE, and the runner-up is why. If
#    libgcrypt were ever dropped from this tree the search would fall through to
#    `libcrypto`, which is taken as a BARE cc.find_library('crypto') with no
#    version check at all. Two openssl majors are live in this repository's
#    index and CI seeds a different one than tape's resolver picks, so an
#    unversioned -lcrypto is the exact shape that produces a binary linking
#    libcrypto.so.3 on a system that ships only .so.4. libgcrypt has one major
#    here and is resolved through pkg-config. The pin is what stops a change in
#    a different package from silently relocating this link.
#
# 2. -Ddri3=true. Direct rendering for X11 clients. Left at `auto`, meson.build
#    339-341 computes it as a conjunction of four things -- dri3proto, xshmfence,
#    libdrm and sys/eventfd.h -- and any one of them missing turns DRI3 off
#    without a word. The result is not a failure: X11 applications fall back to
#    copying every frame through the server, and the symptom arrives months
#    later as "X11 apps feel sluggish". Setting it to true makes dri3proto
#    `required` (meson.build:96) and turns the xshmfence and eventfd branches
#    into explicit error() calls (343-349).
#
# 3. -Dxwayland_ei=portal. Emulated input, i.e. XTEST, which is the path
#    synthetic input takes under a Wayland compositor -- remote desktop typing
#    into an X11 window, and every accessibility tool that injects events.
#    `auto` makes BOTH libei and liboeffis `required: false` (meson.build
#    224-225), so a missing libei yields an Xwayland with no XTEST support and a
#    successful build.
#
#    `portal` rather than `socket`: it makes liboeffis required, which is the
#    RemoteDesktop-portal route rather than a bare socket, and it is what the
#    libei recipe was built -Dliboeffis=enabled for. The value also travels into
#    xwayland.pc as have_enable_ei_portal, which is where mutter reads this
#    package's capabilities from -- so getting it wrong is not only a missing
#    feature, it is a missing feature the compositor is told about.
#
# 4. -Dsystemd_notify=false, AND elogind IS DECLARED ANYWAY. The flag controls
#    the FEATURE, not the LINK. libsystemd is looked up `required: false`
#    (meson.build:107) but sits in the COMMON dependency list unconditionally
#    (meson.build:406), so every configuration of this option produces an
#    artefact that links elogind's libsystemd.pc alias. "Configure found it" and
#    "the artefact links it" are two facts and neither implies the other. The
#    dependency is declared because the second one is true; the flag is false
#    because there is no systemd here to notify.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE ONE WITH NO FLAG AT ALL
# ===========================================================================
#
# GLAMOR IS NOT CONTROLLED BY -Dglamor. Setting it true only compiles the
# glamor/ subdirectory; whether Xwayland USES it is decided by a second,
# unnamed condition (include/meson.build:320):
#
#     build_xwayland_glamor = build_glamor and gbm_dep.found()
#
# and gbm_dep is `dependency('gbm', required: false)` (meson.build:206). So a
# build image whose mesa lost -Dgbm=enabled produces an Xwayland that configures
# green with -Dglamor=true and renders every X11 client on the CPU. There is no
# option that can turn that into an error, which is why install.sh asserts
# XWL_HAS_GLAMOR on the generated header instead. -Dglamor=true is stated below
# because it is half of that conjunction and the half a person can set.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE TWO THAT ARE CHANGED FROM THEIR DEFAULTS
# ===========================================================================
#
# -Dsecure-rpc=false. Default TRUE, and it does not build here: os/meson.build
# 61-66 requires either libtirpc or rpc/rpc.h from libc, glibc removed Sun RPC
# years ago, and libtirpc is not packaged -- so the default is a hard error()
# rather than a silent skip. SUN-DES-1 is a Secure-RPC authentication scheme
# nothing has used this century; this is the one place in the file where the
# right answer is to turn a default off rather than pin it on.
#
# -Dxvfb=false. Default true, and it builds a SECOND X server -- Xvfb, the
# virtual framebuffer (hw/meson.build 1-3). Nothing in a GNOME session runs it.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE REST OF THE AUTOS, PINNED SO THAT THE ANSWER IS NOT THE IMAGE'S
# ===========================================================================
#
# -Dipv6=true            auto probes cc.has_function('getaddrinfo'); glibc has
#                        it, so this states the outcome rather than re-deriving
#                        it in every image.
# -Dinput_thread=true    auto probes pthread_setschedparam. The input thread is
#                        what keeps pointer motion smooth while the server is
#                        busy; losing it is a latency regression with no error.
# -Dmitshm=true          auto probes sys/shm.h. MIT-SHM is how an X11 client
#                        hands the server a pixmap without copying it.
# -Dxselinux=false       auto, and it resolves to false here because neither
#                        libselinux nor audit is packaged -- but by the
#                        CONJUNCTION at meson.build 447-451 rather than by
#                        intent, and a conjunction that happens to fail closed
#                        is not a decision.
# -Dlibdecor=false       auto over libdecor-0, which is not packaged. It draws
#                        window decorations for a ROOTFUL Xwayland; mutter runs
#                        it rootless.
# -Ddocs=false, -Ddevel-docs=false, -Ddocs-pdf=false
#                        all three auto, each reaching for xorg-sgml-doctools,
#                        xmlto, xsltproc or fop. Same family as libnotify's
#                        -Dman=true: an option that names a tool usually reaches
#                        for more than the tool.
#
# -Dglx=true and -Dxdmcp=true are stated at their defaults because both are
# deterministic here and both are things a reader will wonder about. GLX is
# server-side indirect rendering built from this tarball's own sources through
# epoxy -- it does NOT need the libGL that mesa deliberately does not build
# (mesa is -Dglx=disabled, and that is the CLIENT-side GLX). xdmcp resolves
# libXdmcp, which is packaged.
#
# NOT PASSED, DELIBERATELY:
#   -Ddrm      the option exists in meson_options.txt and is referenced nowhere
#              in xwayland 24.1.8 -- an Xorg-only leftover. libdrm is taken
#              through the glamor branch instead.
#   -Dxkb_dir, -Dxkb_bin_dir
#              both default to '' and are resolved from xkbcomp.pc
#              (meson.build 116-135), falling back to prefix-derived literals
#              that are the SAME values. Pinning them would make the two agree
#              by decree and hide whether xkbcomp was there at all; install.sh
#              asserts the resolved values instead.
#   -Dxkb_output_dir
#              defaults to <xkb_dir>/compiled, which nothing creates. That is
#              not a gap: xkb/ddxLoad.c 72-81 falls back to $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and
#              then to /tmp when it is not writable. Naming a directory no
#              package creates would state a fact that is not true.
MESON_ARGS="-Dsha1=libgcrypt \
-Ddri3=true \
-Dxwayland_ei=portal \
-Dsystemd_notify=false \
-Dglamor=true \
-Dsecure-rpc=false \
-Dxvfb=false \
-Dipv6=true \
-Dinput_thread=true \
-Dmitshm=true \
-Dxselinux=false \
-Dlibdecor=false \
-Ddocs=false \
-Ddevel-docs=false \
-Ddocs-pdf=false \
-Dglx=true \
-Dxdmcp=true"
